# Issue #962. The Heritage `value of … for key …` reader (`spec/grammar.md:219`'s
# `value-of-reader`) is ONE expression, so `spec/grammar.md:34` — "Within a single expression, list
# literal, dict literal, or parenthesized group, newlines are insignificant" — governs the slots
# inside it. Before the fix the reader stopped at a newline before its required `for key` tail, so
# each spelling below raised `ol-bad-token`, and the parenthesized one additionally raised
# `ol-unmatched-paren` TWICE on parentheses that are correctly matched.
#
# This fixture pins the CLEAN half. Its execution twin,
# `heritage/execution/heritage-value-of-key-reader-across-a-newline`, pins that these spellings also
# MEAN what their one-line spellings mean — which matters most for the dict rows, where the broken
# reading additionally produced an entry keyed `key` in place of the entry that was written, a
# misreading no diagnostic described.
#
# The reader operates on a dict, so Heritage also needs Data (`spec/conformance.md:305`).
:d = { a: 7 b: "a" }
:k = "a"

# The bare reader split before its tail.
print value of :d
for key :k

# Wrapped in parentheses — the spelling that raised the phantom `ol-unmatched-paren` pair.
print (value of :d
for key :k)

# Inside a list literal.
print [value of :d
for key :k]

# Inside a dict literal, the entry VALUE.
print { a: value of :d
for key :k }

# The reader's other internal slots take a newline too: after `of`, between `for` and `key`, and
# after `key`.
print value of
:d for key :k
print value of :d for
key :k
print value of :d for key
:k

# The same three slots INSIDE a dict entry value. This is a cross-product, not a repetition: a
# dictionary is the one context where a newline separates entries, so a continuation guarded by the
# dict-key test behaves differently here than it does at the top level.
print { a: value of
:d for key :k }
print { a: value of :d for
key :k }
print { a: value of :d for key
:k }

# A newline on BOTH sides of `for`, at the top level and inside a dict entry value. The tail's two
# words are looked up past newlines of their own, so this is still one expression.
print value of :d
for
key :k
print { a: value of :d
for
key :k }

# Case is the third axis of the cross-product. Keywords are case-insensitive
# (`spec/grammar.md:13`), so an UPPERCASE or mixed-case tail must continue across a newline exactly
# as the lowercase one does — a continuation comparing surface text would break only these rows.
#
# No diagnostic appears, but NOT because this fixture leaves `style` off: measured, turning `style`
# on changes nothing here. `ol-style-name-case` fires only on the reader's head `value`, which is
# lowercase in every row; `of`/`for`/`key` have no span of their own and are not linted at all,
# deferred to the #115 follow-up. `heritage/style/name-case-uppercase-value-of-key-reader` records
# that, and is where the head's lint is pinned.
print value of :d
FOR KEY :k
print value of :d
For Key :k
print { a: value of :d
FOR key :k }

# Blank lines and a comment line are whitespace inside the expression like any other.
print value of :d

# a comment between the operand and the tail
for key :k

# Controls — the one-line spellings, clean before the fix and still clean after it.
print value of :d for key :k
print (value of :d for key :k)
print [value of :d for key :k]
print { a: value of :d for key :k }

# Control: arithmetic already continued across a newline inside a dict (issues #933/#956); this row
# is what makes the failures above reader-specific rather than a general expression regression.
print { a: 1
+ 2 }

# Control: `for` and `key` are legal dictionary keys, and inside a dict a newline separates
# ENTRIES — so the reader's continuation must not claim a following entry that merely begins with
# one of its tail words.
print { a: 1
key: 2
for: 3 }

# Control: a newline still terminates a statement.
print 1
print 2
